Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): First, it authenticates the parties communicating and messages between them. This helps protect against impersonation, duplicity, and false denial and other compromises by providing a way to establish that a message really comes from one party it claims to come from and is intended for the true party receiving it.

175
00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:54.850
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Second, T-Authenticates is a universal trust interoperability layer. It accepts any verifiable identifiers that may be established by any organizations, communities, or jurisdictions, and provides the expected authenticity assurances about the parties and the messages.

